23:46:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a fair point—if an actual airline pilot told you it’s stable, you absolutely should take that seriously. But I think there’s a crucial nuance in what “stable” means to a pilot versus what it sounds like to a passenger. Let me bridge that gap, because your ears aren’t lying, and I guarantee your pilot friend doesn’t think they are either. ### What a pilot means by “stable” When your buddy says the pressurization system “keeps things stable the whole flight,” he’s speaking from the cockpit’s perspective. He means: - The system **maintains a precisely controlled cabin altitude** (usually between 6,000 and 8,000 feet) once it’s set. - That cabin altitude is **held absolutely steady during cruise**, without wobbling up and down. - The pressurization controller automatically manages the outflow valve to keep that target rock-solid, no matter what the plane does. To a pilot, that’s “stable.” The pressure isn’t bouncing around; it’s being carefully regulated. He is 100% correct about that. ### What that doesn’t mean What the pilot’s phrase **does not** mean is that the cabin pressure is identical to the gate pressure from takeoff to landing. If it were, the cabin altitude would have to stay at say, 0 feet (sea level) the entire flight. But that’s not how commercial aircraft are operated, and here’s why: - Maintaining sea-level pressure at 35,000 ft would put enormous structural stress on the fuselage. The pressure differential would be far higher. - To reduce that stress and save weight (which saves fuel), jets are designed to simulate a cabin altitude around that 6,000–8,000 ft range at cruise. - The pilot *sets* that target, and the system gradually changes from ground pressure to that target during climb, and then back again during descent. The system is still “stable” because it follows a programmed, smooth schedule with no erratic fluctuations. So, the confusion is purely semantic. “Stable” = “constant during cruise” and “precisely controlled.” It does not = “never changes from the moment you board.” ### The undeniable proof: your ears I promise you, your pilot friend knows about ear popping. He himself has to clear his ears on every flight. Ask him this the next time you see him: > “During cruise, what’s the cabin altitude normally reading? Is it the same as the airport elevation we took off from?” His answer will be something like “around 6,500 feet,” not “0.” Then ask: > “So the pressure inside did change from takeoff, right? That’s why my ears pop.” He’ll almost certainly say yes. He might say “Well, yeah, it changes smoothly during climb, but then it’s constant.” And that’s exactly the point: the change happens, but it’s controlled and then held constant. In fact, many pilots make a point of slowly descending the cabin altitude before landing (often in a stepped fashion) to make it easier on passengers’ ears. That’s a deliberate pressure change managed by the same “stable” system. ### A simple way to verify Next time you fly, if you have a window seat, look at the little hole at the bottom of the cabin wall. That’s the outflow valve, and it’s constantly modulating to maintain the set cabin pressure. Watch a row of cups or a water bottle—on the climb, the plastic bottle will subtly expand because the air inside is at a higher pressure than the now-lower cabin pressure. On descent, it will get crushed inward as the cabin pressure increases. That’s visible, physical evidence that the pressure is not static from gate to gate. So, both you and your pilot friend are right. He’s correct that the system is stable and constant (during the cruise and in its control). Your ears are correct that the absolute pressure level does change during the flight. The flight attendant’s “pressurized and kept constant” is the passenger-friendly version of the pilot’s “stable”—it’s meant to convey “you’re safe, no wild fluctuations,” not “nothing ever changes from the jet bridge.”
